{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "from math import isqrt\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly, smaller than n.\n    Returns 1 if n is prime.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(2, isqrt(n) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return n // i\n    return 1"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "To implement the software design specification, I will generate the necessary files and their contents, structured according to the `File Structure` section.\n\n```\n# pyproject.toml\n[tool.poetry]\nname = \"numberutils\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A utility to compute the largest proper divisor of a positive integer.\"\nauthors = [\"Your Name <you@example.com>\"]\nreadme = \"README.md\"\npackages = [{include = \"numberutils\", from = \"src\"}]\n\n[tool.poetry.dependencies]\npython = \">=3.9,<3.12\"\n\n[tool.poetry.group.dev.dependencies]\npytest = \"^8.0.0\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"poetry-core\"]\nbuild-backend = \"poetry.core.masonry.api\"\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\npythonpath = \"src\"\n\n[tool.poetry.scripts]\nnumberutils = \"numberutils.main:run_cli\"\n```\n\n```\n# README.md\n# NumberUtils\n\nA Python utility that computes the largest proper divisor of a positive integer.\n\n## Features\n\n-   **Library API**: Easily integrate the largest divisor computation into your Python programs.\n-   **CLI Entrypoint**: A command-line interface for quick computations.\n-   **Automated Tests**: Comprehensive unit and integration tests ensure reliability.\n-   **CI Configuration**: GitHub Actions workflow to detect regressions early.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\npip install numberutils\n```\n(Note: Replace `numberutils` with your package name if you publish it)\n\nOr, if developing:\n\n```bash\ngit clone <your-repo-url>\ncd numberutils\npoetry install\n```\n\n## Usage\n\n### Command Line Interface (CLI)\n\nTo compute the largest proper divisor using the CLI:\n\n```bash\npython -m numberutils 15\n# Output: Largest divisor of 15 is 5\n\npython -m numberutils 7\n# Output: Largest divisor of 7 is 1\n\npython -m numberutils 1\n# Output: n must be greater than 1\n# Exit code: 2\n```\n\nUsing the installed console script (if `pyproject.toml` is configured for it and installed via pip):\n\n```bash\nnumberutils 20\n# Output: Largest divisor of 20 is 10\n```\n\n### Library API\n\nYou can use the `LargestDivisorService` directly in your Python code:\n\n```python\nfrom numberutils.service import LargestDivisorService\nfrom numberutils.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nservice = LargestDivisorService()\n\ntry:\n    divisor = service.largest_divisor(15)\n    print(f\"Largest divisor of 15 is {divisor}\") # Output: Largest divisor of 15 is 5\n\n    divisor = service.largest_divisor(49)\n    print(f\"Largest divisor of 49 is {divisor}\") # Output: Largest divisor of 49 is 7\n\n    divisor = service.largest_divisor(1)\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\") # Output: Error: n must be > 1\n```\n\n## Development\n\n### Running Tests\n\nTo run the automated tests:\n\n```bash\npoetry run pytest\n```\n\n### CI/CD\n\nThe project includes a GitHub Actions workflow (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) that runs tests on pushes and pull requests for various Python versions.\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/__init__.py\nfrom .main import MainApp, run_cli\nfrom .controller import CLIController\nfrom .service import LargestDivisorService\nfrom .validator import InputValidator\nfrom .dto import LargestDivisorResult\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"MainApp\",\n    \"run_cli\",\n    \"CLIController\",\n    \"LargestDivisorService\",\n    \"InputValidator\",\n    \"LargestDivisorResult\",\n    \"InvalidInputError\",\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/__main__.py\nimport sys\nfrom .main import MainApp\n\ndef run_module(argv=None):\n    \"\"\"\n    Module-level CLI entry to run package as \"python -m numberutils\".\n    It delegates to MainApp.run.\n    \"\"\"\n    argv = argv if argv is not None else sys.argv\n    app = MainApp()\n    return app.run(argv)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(run_module())\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/main.py\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .service import LargestDivisorService\nfrom .validator import InputValidator\nfrom .controller import CLIController\n\nclass MainApp:\n    \"\"\"\n    Application bootstrap and top-level orchestration for the CLI.\n    Constructs dependencies and delegates to the CLIController.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        service: Optional[LargestDivisorService] = None,\n        validator: Optional[InputValidator] = None,\n        controller: Optional[CLIController] = None\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Constructs the application; uses dependency injection for testability.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.service = service or LargestDivisorService()\n        self.validator = validator or InputValidator()\n        self.controller = controller or CLIController(self.service, self.validator)\n\n    def run(self, argv: List[str]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Orchestrates handling of argv, calls controller.handle, prints outputs.\n        Returns process exit code (0 for success, non-zero for error).\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            exit_code, output = self.controller.handle(argv)\n            if exit_code == 0:\n                print(output)\n            else:\n                print(output, file=sys.stderr)\n            return exit_code\n        except Exception as e:\n            # Catch any unexpected errors that were not handled by the controller\n            print(f\"An unexpected internal error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1\n\ndef run_cli():\n    \"\"\"Entry point for console scripts defined in pyproject.toml.\"\"\"\n    app = MainApp()\n    sys.exit(app.run(sys.argv))\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/controller.py\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\nfrom .service import LargestDivisorService\nfrom .validator import InputValidator\nfrom .dto import LargestDivisorResult\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"\n    Handles CLI parsing, input validation, service calls, and formatting of results or errors.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: LargestDivisorService, validator: InputValidator) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes the controller with required service and validator dependencies.\"\"\"\n        self.service = service\n        self.validator = validator\n\n    def handle(self, argv: List[str]) -> Tuple[int, str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Parses CLI arguments, validates input, computes the divisor, and returns\n        an exit code and a message (stdout or error).\n        \"\"\"\n        if len(argv) < 2:\n            return 2, \"Usage: python -m numberutils <integer>\"\n        try:\n            n = self.validator.validate_int(argv[1])\n            divisor = self.service.largest_divisor(n)\n            # The design states service returns an int, controller builds DTO if needed.\n            # For CLI, a simple string output is sufficient per FR3.\n            # If a DTO was explicitly required for CLI output, we'd do LargestDivisorResult(n, divisor).\n            return 0, f\"Largest divisor of {n} is {divisor}\"\n        except InvalidInputError as e:\n            return 2, str(e)\n        except Exception as e:\n            # Catch any unexpected errors during service call not caught by specific InvalidInputError\n            return 1, f\"An unexpected error occurred during computation: {e}\"\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/service.py\nfrom math import isqrt\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass LargestDivisorService:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the largest proper divisor of a given positive integer n.\n    A proper divisor of n is a positive divisor of n other than n itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    MAX_COMPUTE_N: int = 10**18  # Safety cap for computation to prevent excessive load\n\n    def largest_divisor(self, n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Finds the largest integer 'd' such that 1 <= d < n and n % d == 0.\n        Raises InvalidInputError if n is not an integer or n <= 1 or n > MAX_COMPUTE_N.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"n must be an integer\")\n        if n <= 1:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"n must be > 1\")\n        if n > self.MAX_COMPUTE_N:\n            raise InvalidInputError(f\"n too large (>{self.MAX_COMPUTE_N})\")\n\n        # Fast path for even numbers: n // 2 is always the largest proper divisor\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return n // 2\n\n        max_proper_divisor = 1 # Initialize with 1, which is the largest proper divisor for primes\n        limit = isqrt(n)\n\n        # Iterate from 1 up to sqrt(n) to find divisors\n        for i in range(1, limit + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                # i is a divisor\n                # n // i is also a divisor\n                \n                # Consider i: it must be less than n to be a proper divisor\n                if i < n and i > max_proper_divisor:\n                    max_proper_divisor = i\n                \n                # Consider the pair n // i: it must be less than n to be a proper divisor\n                pair = n // i\n                if pair < n and pair > max_proper_divisor:\n                    max_proper_divisor = pair\n        \n        return max_proper_divisor\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/validator.py\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates and coerces string inputs into domain integers, enforcing basic constraints.\n    \"\"\"\n    def validate_int(self, value: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Attempts to convert a string value to an integer and validates its range.\n        Raises InvalidInputError for non-integer strings or if the integer is <= 1.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            n = int(value)\n        except ValueError:\n            raise InvalidInputError(f\"Invalid input '{value}'. n must be an integer.\")\n        \n        if n <= 1:\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"n must be greater than 1.\")\n        \n        return n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/dto.py\nfrom typing import Dict\n\nclass LargestDivisorResult:\n    \"\"\"\n    A simple data transfer object (DTO) to hold the input number and its largest proper divisor.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, n: int, divisor: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes the result object with the input number and its computed divisor.\"\"\"\n        self.n = n\n        self.divisor = divisor\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, int]:\n        \"\"\"Converts the result object into a dictionary format.\"\"\"\n        return {\"n\": self.n, \"divisor\": self.divisor}\n```\n\n```python\n# src/numberutils/exceptions.py\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"\n    Custom exception for domain-specific invalid input scenarios.\n    Used when input fails validation rules.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes the exception with a specific error message.\"\"\"\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(message)\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Returns the error message as the string representation of the exception.\"\"\"\n        return self.message\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_service.py\nimport pytest\nfrom numberutils.service import LargestDivisorService\nfrom numberutils.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef service():\n    \"\"\"Provides a LargestDivisorService instance for tests.\"\"\"\n    return LargestDivisorService()\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_composite_odd(service):\n    \"\"\"Test with an odd composite number.\"\"\"\n    assert service.largest_divisor(15) == 5\n    assert service.largest_divisor(21) == 7\n    assert service.largest_divisor(49) == 7\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_composite_even(service):\n    \"\"\"Test with an even composite number.\"\"\"\n    assert service.largest_divisor(10) == 5\n    assert service.largest_divisor(12) == 6\n    assert service.largest_divisor(100) == 50\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_prime(service):\n    \"\"\"Test with prime numbers.\"\"\"\n    assert service.largest_divisor(2) == 1\n    assert service.largest_divisor(3) == 1\n    assert service.largest_divisor(7) == 1\n    assert service.largest_divisor(13) == 1\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_power_of_prime(service):\n    \"\"\"Test with powers of prime numbers.\"\"\"\n    assert service.largest_divisor(4) == 2\n    assert service.largest_divisor(9) == 3\n    assert service.largest_divisor(25) == 5\n    assert service.largest_divisor(81) == 27 # Largest proper divisor of 3^4 (81) is 3^3 (27)\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_large_prime(service):\n    \"\"\"Test with a large prime number.\"\"\"\n    # 1,000,000,007 is a prime number\n    assert service.largest_divisor(1000000007) == 1\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_large_composite(service):\n    \"\"\"Test with a large composite number.\"\"\"\n    # Example large composite: 10^12 - 1 = (10^6 - 1)(10^6 + 1)\n    # This is 999999999999 = 3 * 3 * 7 * 11 * 13 * 37 * 101 * 9901\n    # Smallest factor is 3, so largest proper divisor is N/3\n    n = 999999999999\n    expected_divisor = n // 3\n    assert service.largest_divisor(n) == expected_divisor\n\n    # Another large composite: 10^12 (even)\n    n_even = 10**12\n    assert service.largest_divisor(n_even) == n_even // 2\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_n_equals_one_raises_error(service):\n    \"\"\"Test that n=1 raises InvalidInputError.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be > 1\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(1)\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_n_equals_zero_raises_error(service):\n    \"\"\"Test that n=0 raises InvalidInputError.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be > 1\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(0)\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_negative_n_raises_error(service):\n    \"\"\"Test that negative n raises InvalidInputError.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be > 1\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(-5)\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_non_integer_n_raises_error(service):\n    \"\"\"Test that non-integer n raises InvalidInputError.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be an integer\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(15.0)\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be an integer\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(\"abc\")\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be an integer\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(None)\n\ndef test_largest_divisor_n_above_max_compute_n_raises_error(service):\n    \"\"\"Test that n above MAX_COMPUTE_N raises InvalidInputError.\"\"\"\n    n_too_large = service.MAX_COMPUTE_N + 1\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=f\"n too large (>{service.MAX_COMPUTE_N})\"):\n        service.largest_divisor(n_too_large)\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_validator.py\nimport pytest\nfrom numberutils.validator import InputValidator\nfrom numberutils.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef validator():\n    \"\"\"Provides an InputValidator instance for tests.\"\"\"\n    return InputValidator()\n\ndef test_validate_int_valid_positive_integer(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test valid positive integer strings.\"\"\"\n    assert validator.validate_int(\"2\") == 2\n    assert validator.validate_int(\"100\") == 100\n    assert validator.validate_int(\"999999999999\") == 999999999999\n\ndef test_validate_int_n_equals_one_raises_error(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test that '1' raises an error as per spec (n > 1).\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be greater than 1\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"1\")\n\ndef test_validate_int_n_equals_zero_raises_error(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test that '0' raises an error.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be greater than 1\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"0\")\n\ndef test_validate_int_negative_integer_raises_error(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test that negative integers raise an error.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be greater than 1\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"-5\")\n\ndef test_validate_int_non_numeric_string_raises_error(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test that non-numeric strings raise an error.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Invalid input 'abc'. n must be an integer.\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"abc\")\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Invalid input ''. n must be an integer.\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"\")\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Invalid input ' '. n must be an integer.\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\" \")\n\ndef test_validate_int_float_string_raises_error(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test that float strings raise an error.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Invalid input '15.0'. n must be an integer.\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"15.0\")\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Invalid input '3.14'. n must be an integer.\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\"3.14\")\n\ndef test_validate_int_string_with_whitespace(validator):\n    \"\"\"Test strings with leading/trailing whitespace (should still parse if valid).\"\"\"\n    assert validator.validate_int(\" 10 \") == 10\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"n must be greater than 1\"):\n        validator.validate_int(\" 1 \")\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_cli.py\nimport pytest\nimport sys\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom numberutils.main import MainApp\nfrom numberutils.service import LargestDivisorService\nfrom numberutils.validator import InputValidator\nfrom numberutils.controller import CLIController\nfrom numberutils.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef mock_stdout_stderr():\n    \"\"\"Captures stdout and stderr for testing CLI output.\"\"\"\n    with patch('sys.stdout', new_callable=StringIO) as mock_out:\n        with patch('sys.stderr', new_callable=StringIO) as mock_err:\n            yield mock_out, mock_err\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef app_with_mocked_deps():\n    \"\"\"Provides a MainApp instance with mocked dependencies for isolation.\"\"\"\n    mock_service = LargestDivisorService()\n    mock_validator = InputValidator()\n    mock_controller = CLIController(mock_service, mock_validator)\n    return MainApp(service=mock_service, validator=mock_validator, controller=mock_controller)\n\ndef test_cli_success(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test successful CLI execution with valid input.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"15\"]\n    exit_code = app_with_mocked_deps.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 0\n    assert \"Largest divisor of 15 is 5\" in mock_out.getvalue()\n    assert mock_err.getvalue() == \"\"\n\ndef test_cli_prime_number(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test CLI with a prime number.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"7\"]\n    exit_code = app_with_mocked_deps.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 0\n    assert \"Largest divisor of 7 is 1\" in mock_out.getvalue()\n    assert mock_err.getvalue() == \"\"\n\ndef test_cli_no_argument(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test CLI with no arguments.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n    args = [\"numberutils\"]\n    exit_code = app_with_mocked_deps.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 2\n    assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n    assert \"Usage: python -m numberutils <integer>\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n\ndef test_cli_invalid_integer_string(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test CLI with a non-numeric argument.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"abc\"]\n    exit_code = app_with_mocked_deps.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 2\n    assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n    assert \"Invalid input 'abc'. n must be an integer.\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n\ndef test_cli_n_equals_one(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test CLI with n=1.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"1\"]\n    exit_code = app_with_mocked_deps.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 2\n    assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n    assert \"n must be greater than 1.\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n\ndef test_cli_n_negative(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test CLI with negative n.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"-5\"]\n    exit_code = app_with_mocked_deps.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 2\n    assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n    assert \"n must be greater than 1.\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n\ndef test_cli_unexpected_controller_error(mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test that MainApp handles unexpected errors from controller.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n\n    # Mock controller to raise a generic Exception\n    class MockFailingController(CLIController):\n        def handle(self, argv: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str]:\n            raise ValueError(\"Something went terribly wrong!\")\n\n    failing_controller = MockFailingController(LargestDivisorService(), InputValidator())\n    app = MainApp(controller=failing_controller)\n\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"10\"]\n    exit_code = app.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 1\n    assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n    assert \"An unexpected internal error occurred: Something went terribly wrong!\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n\ndef test_cli_unexpected_service_error(app_with_mocked_deps, mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test CLI handles unexpected errors from service (caught by controller).\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n\n    # Mock service to raise a generic Exception\n    class MockFailingService(LargestDivisorService):\n        def largest_divisor(self, n: int) -> int:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"Service computation failed!\")\n    \n    # Inject the failing service into the controller, and then the app\n    mock_validator = InputValidator()\n    failing_service = MockFailingService()\n    controller_with_failing_service = CLIController(failing_service, mock_validator)\n    app = MainApp(service=failing_service, validator=mock_validator, controller=controller_with_failing_service)\n\n    args = [\"numberutils\", \"10\"]\n    exit_code = app.run(args)\n    assert exit_code == 1\n    assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n    assert \"An unexpected error occurred during computation: Service computation failed!\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n\ndef test_main_module_entrypoint(mock_stdout_stderr):\n    \"\"\"Test the __main__.py entrypoint.\"\"\"\n    mock_out, mock_err = mock_stdout_stderr\n\n    # Use a dummy sys.argv for the test\n    with patch.object(sys, 'argv', ['numberutils', '20']):\n        from numberutils import __main__\n        with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as sysexit:\n            __main__.run_module()\n        assert sysexit.value.code == 0\n        assert \"Largest divisor of 20 is 10\" in mock_out.getvalue()\n        assert mock_err.getvalue() == \"\"\n\n    with patch.object(sys, 'argv', ['numberutils', 'invalid']):\n        from numberutils import __main__\n        with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as sysexit:\n            __main__.run_module()\n        assert sysexit.value.code == 2\n        assert mock_out.getvalue() == \"\"\n        assert \"Invalid input 'invalid'. n must be an integer.\" in mock_err.getvalue()\n```\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/ci.yml\nname: Python CI\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n  pull_request:\n    branches:\n      - main\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        python-version: [\"3.9\", \"3.10\", \"3.11\"]\n\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Set up Python ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n      uses: actions/setup-python@v4\n      with:\n        python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}\n 