{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "```python\ndef anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns an \"ordered\" version of a string by sorting characters inside each word by ASCII value.\n    Word order and all spaces are preserved.\n    \"\"\"\n    SPACE_CHAR = \" \"\n    \n    raw_tokens = s.split(SPACE_CHAR)\n    processed_tokens = []\n    \n    for i, part in enumerate(raw_tokens):\n        if part != \"\":\n            processed_tokens.append(part)\n        \n        # Add a space back if it was a separator between parts, but not after the last part\n        if i < len(raw_tokens) - 1:\n            processed_tokens.append(SPACE_CHAR)\n            \n    sorted_words = []\n    for token in processed_tokens:\n        if token == SPACE_CHAR:\n            sorted_words.append(token)\n        else:\n            sorted_words.append(\"\".join(sorted(token)))\n\n    return \"\".join(sorted_words)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "def anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a string where characters within each word are sorted by ASCII value.\n    Word order and exact spacing are preserved from the original sentence.\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = s.split(' ')\n    processed_tokens = []\n    for token in tokens:\n        if token == '':\n            processed_tokens.append('')\n        else:\n            processed_tokens.append(''.join(sorted(token, key=ord)))\n    return ' '.join(processed_tokens)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "def get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds occurrences of an integer x in a 2D nested list and returns coordinates.\n    Results are sorted by row ascending and column descending.\n    \"\"\"\n    matches = []\n    for r_idx, row in enumerate(lst):\n        for c_idx, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val == x:\n                matches.append((r_idx, c_idx))\n    \n    # Sort by row ascending (c[0]) and then by column descending (-c[1])\n    return sorted(matches, key=lambda c: (c[0], -c[1]))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "def get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds occurrences of x in a ragged 2D list and returns coordinates.\n    Coordinates are sorted by row ascending, then by column descending.\n    \"\"\"\n    found_coordinates = []\n    for r, row in enumerate(lst):\n        for c, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val == x:\n                found_coordinates.append((r, c))\n\n    # Sort coordinates by row ascending, and within a row by column descending.\n    # A tuple key (row, -column) achieves this with a single sort operation.\n    found_coordinates.sort(key=lambda coord: (coord[0], -coord[1]))\n\n    return found_coordinates"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "def sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts an array of non-negative integers based on the parity of the sum of its first and last elements.\n    Returns a new sorted array without modifying the original.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not array:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a copy of the input array.\n    arr_copy = list(array)\n\n    first_element = arr_copy[0]\n    last_element = arr_copy[-1]\n    elements_sum = first_element + last_element\n\n    if elements_sum % 2 == 1:  # Odd sum, sort ascending\n        return sorted(arr_copy)\n    else:  # Even sum, sort descending\n        return sorted(arr_copy, reverse=True)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of non-negative integers, this function returns a sorted copy without modifying the original.\n    Sorting is ascending if the sum of first and last elements is odd, and descending if even.\n    \"\"\"\n    new_array = list(array)\n\n    if not new_array or len(new_array) == 1:\n        return new_array\n\n    first_val = new_array[0]\n    last_val = new_array[-1]\n    total_sum = first_val + last_val\n\n    if total_sum % 2 == 1:  # Odd sum\n        new_array.sort(reverse=False)\n    else:  # Even sum\n        new_array.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    return new_array\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/89", "solution": "To implement the provided software design specification, here is the complete, executable code organized by file as described.\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/exceptions.py`\n```python\nclass EncryptError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for encryption-related errors.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str, inner: Exception | None = None) -> None:\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n        self.inner = inner\n\n```\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/models.py`\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass EncryptRequest:\n    \"\"\"Data transfer object for encryption requests.\"\"\"\n    text: str\n    shift: int | None = None\n\n@dataclass\nclass EncryptResult:\n    \"\"\"Data transfer object for encryption results.\"\"\"\n    original: str\n    encrypted: str\n    shift: int\n\n```\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/utils.py`\n```python\nALPHABET_LOWER: str = \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\"\nALPHABET_UPPER: str = \"ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\"\nALPHABET_SIZE: int = 26\n\nclass AlphabetUtils:\n    \"\"\"Utility functions for alphabet-based character rotation.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def rotate_char(char: str, shift: int) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Rotates an ASCII letter by a given shift, preserving case. Non-letters are returned unchanged.\"\"\"\n        if char.islower():\n            return AlphabetUtils._rotate_with_alphabet(char, shift, ALPHABET_LOWER)\n        if char.isupper():\n            return AlphabetUtils._rotate_with_alphabet(char, shift, ALPHABET_UPPER)\n        return char\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _rotate_with_alphabet(char: str, shift: int, alphabet: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Helper to perform rotation for a specific alphabet string.\"\"\"\n        idx = alphabet.index(char)\n        new_idx = (idx + shift) % ALPHABET_SIZE\n        return alphabet[new_idx]\n\n```\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/services.py`\n```python\nfrom rotcipher.exceptions import EncryptError\nfrom rotcipher.models import EncryptRequest, EncryptResult\nfrom rotcipher.utils import AlphabetUtils\n\nclass EncryptService:\n    \"\"\"Provides encryption business logic and input validation.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, default_shift: int = 4) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes the service with a default rotation shift.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(default_shift, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"default_shift must be an integer.\")\n        self.default_shift = default_shift\n        self.utils = AlphabetUtils()\n\n    def encrypt(self, text: str, shift: int | None = None) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Encrypts a string by rotating alphabet letters. Preserves case and non-alphabet characters.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(text, str):\n            raise EncryptError(\"Input 'text' must be a string.\", TypeError(f\"Expected str, got {type(text)}\"))\n        \n        effective_shift = self.default_shift if shift is None else shift\n        if not isinstance(effective_shift, int):\n            raise EncryptError(\"Shift value must be an integer.\", ValueError(f\"Expected int, got {type(effective_shift)}\"))\n        \n        return \"\".join(self.utils.rotate_char(c, effective_shift) for c in text)\n\n    def encrypt_request(self, request: EncryptRequest) -> EncryptResult:\n        \"\"\"Encrypts based on an EncryptRequest DTO, returning an EncryptResult DTO.\"\"\"\n        effective_shift = self.default_shift if request.shift is None else request.shift\n        encrypted_text = self.encrypt(request.text, effective_shift)\n        return EncryptResult(original=request.text, encrypted=encrypted_text, shift=effective_shift)\n\n```\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/controllers.py`\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport sys\n\nfrom rotcipher.exceptions import EncryptError\nfrom rotcipher.services import EncryptService\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"Handles command-line interface orchestration and argument parsing.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, service: EncryptService) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Initializes the CLI controller with an EncryptService instance.\"\"\"\n        self.service = service\n\n    def run(self, args: list[str]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Parses CLI arguments, invokes the encryption service, and prints the result.\"\"\"\n        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n            prog=\"rotcipher\",\n            description=\"Encrypts text using a Caesar cipher (rotation cipher).\"\n        )\n        parser.add_argument(\n            \"-t\", \"--text\",\n            required=True,\n            help=\"The string to encrypt.\"\n        )\n        parser.add_argument(\n            \"-s\", \"--shift\",\n            type=int,\n            required=False,\n            help=f\"The amount to shift alphabet letters by (default: {self.service.default_shift}).\"\n        )\n        parser.add_argument(\n            \"-v\", \"--version\",\n            action=\"version\",\n            version=\"rotcipher 0.1.0\" # Placeholder version\n        )\n\n        try:\n            ns = parser.parse_args(args)\n            encrypted = self.service.encrypt(ns.text, ns.shift)\n            print(encrypted)\n            return 0\n        except EncryptError as e:\n            print(f\"Error: {e.message}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1\n        except SystemExit as e:\n            # argparse raises SystemExit on --help or parse errors\n            if e.code == 0:\n                return 0 # Help message printed successfully\n            print(f\"Argument Error: Invalid command line arguments. {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1\n\n```\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/cli.py`\n```python\nimport sys\n\nfrom rotcipher.controllers import CLIController\nfrom rotcipher.services import EncryptService\n\ndef main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"The main entry point for the rotcipher CLI application.\"\"\"\n    argv = sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv\n    \n    service = EncryptService()\n    controller = CLIController(service)\n    \n    return controller.run(argv)\n\n```\n\n---\n`src/rotcipher/__init__.py`\n```python\n\"\"\"\nRotcipher: A Python library and CLI for text encryption via alphabet rotation.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom rotcipher.exceptions import EncryptError\nfrom rotcipher.models import EncryptRequest, EncryptResult\nfrom rotcipher.utils import AlphabetUtils\nfrom rotcipher.services import EncryptService\nfrom rotcipher.controllers import CLIController\nfrom rotcipher.cli import main\n\n# Define what is exposed when the package is imported\n__all__ = [\n    \"EncryptError\",\n    \"EncryptRequest\",\n    \"EncryptResult\",\n    \"AlphabetUtils\",\n    \"EncryptService\",\n    \"CLIController\",\n    \"main\"\n]\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/89", "solution": "```python\ndef encrypt(s):\n    \"\"\"Encrypts a string using a Caesar cipher with a fixed shift of +4.\n    Non-alphabetic characters are preserved, and letter case is maintained.\"\"\"\n    result_chars = []\n    shift = 4  # The alphabet is rotated by 2 * 2 = 4 places.\n\n    for char in s:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            # Handle lowercase letters, applying the shift and wrapping around.\n            start_ord = ord('a')\n            shifted_ord = (ord(char) - start_ord + shift) % 26 + start_ord\n            result_chars.append(chr(shifted_ord))\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':\n            # Handle uppercase letters, applying the shift and wrapping around.\n            start_ord = ord('A')\n            shifted_ord = (ord(char) - start_ord + shift) % 26 + start_ord\n            result_chars.append(chr(shifted_ord))\n        else:\n            # Non-alphabetic characters are appended without alteration.\n            result_chars.append(char)\n\n    return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```"}
