{"task_id": "HumanEval/117", "solution": "def select_words(s, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns words from string s that contain exactly n consonants.\n    Assumes s contains only letters and spaces, and n is a non-negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    VOWELS = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'}\n\n    def _is_consonant(char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is a consonant.\"\"\"\n        return char.isalpha() and char.lower() not in VOWELS\n\n    def _tokenize(text):\n        \"\"\"Splits text into words, handling multiple spaces.\"\"\"\n        return text.split()\n\n    result_words = []\n    tokens = _tokenize(s)\n\n    for word_text in tokens:\n        current_consonant_count = 0\n        for char in word_text:\n            if _is_consonant(char):\n                current_consonant_count += 1\n        \n        if current_consonant_count == n:\n            result_words.append(word_text)\n            \n    return result_words"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/117", "solution": "def select_words(s, n):\n    \"\"\"Returns words from string s that contain exactly n consonants.\n    Vowels are 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u' (case-insensitive); 'y' is a consonant.\n    \"\"\"\n    VOWELS = \"aeiou\"\n    result = []\n    \n    # Split string into words, ignoring consecutive spaces.\n    words = [word for word in s.split() if word]\n\n    for word in words:\n        consonant_count = 0\n        for char in word:\n            lower_char = char.lower()\n            if lower_char.isalpha() and lower_char not in VOWELS:\n                consonant_count += 1\n        \n        if consonant_count == n:\n            result.append(word)\n            \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/118", "solution": "```python\ndef get_closest_vowel(word):\n    \"\"\"Finds the closest vowel that stands between two consonants from the right side of the word.\n    Returns an empty string if no such vowel is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    VOWELS = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u', 'A', 'E', 'I', 'O', 'U'}\n\n    def _is_vowel(char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is a vowel.\"\"\"\n        return char in VOWELS\n\n    def _is_consonant(char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is a consonant.\n        Assumes input characters are English letters only as per requirements.\n        \"\"\"\n        return char not in VOWELS\n\n    word_length = len(word)\n    if word_length < 3:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Iterate from right to left, skipping the first and last characters.\n    # The valid indices to check are from word_length - 2 down to 1.\n    for i in range(word_length - 2, 0, -1):\n        current_char = word[i]\n        left_char = word[i - 1]\n        right_char = word[i + 1]\n\n        if (_is_vowel(current_char) and\n                _is_consonant(left_char) and\n                _is_consonant(right_char)):\n            return current_char\n\n    return \"\"\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/119", "solution": "```python\ndef match_parens(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two parenthesis strings can form a balanced string by concatenation.\n    Returns 'Yes' if possible, 'No' otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _analyze_string(s):\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the net balance and minimal prefix balance of a parenthesis string.\n        \"\"\"\n        balance = 0\n        min_balance = 0\n        for char in s:\n            if char == '(':\n                balance += 1\n            else: # char == ')'\n                balance -= 1\n            if balance < min_balance:\n                min_balance = balance\n        return balance, min_balance\n\n    def _can_concatenate_order(s1, s2):\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if s1 followed by s2 forms a balanced parenthesis string.\n        \"\"\"\n        net1, min_prefix1 = _analyze_string(s1)\n        net2, min_prefix2 = _analyze_string(s2)\n\n        # A combined string S1+S2 is balanced if:\n        # 1. The prefix balance of S1 never drops below zero.\n        # 2. The combined balance (net1 + prefix_balance_of_S2) never drops below zero.\n        # 3. The total net balance of S1 and S2 combined is zero.\n        return min_prefix1 >= 0 and (net1 + min_prefix2 >= 0) and (net1 + net2 == 0)\n\n    # Assuming 'lst' contains exactly two strings as per the problem statement.\n    s_a, s_b = lst\n\n    if _can_concatenate_order(s_a, s_b) or _can_concatenate_order(s_b, s_a):\n        return 'Yes'\n    else:\n        return 'No'\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/118", "solution": "_VOWELS_SET = {\"a\", \"e\", \"i\", \"o\", \"u\", \"A\", \"E\", \"I\", \"O\", \"U\"}\n\ndef _is_vowel(char: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a character is an English vowel (case-sensitive).\"\"\"\n    return char in _VOWELS_SET\n\ndef _is_consonant(char: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a character is an English consonant (case-sensitive).\"\"\"\n    # Assumption from problem statement: input contains English letters only.\n    return char not in _VOWELS_SET\n\ndef get_closest_vowel(word: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Finds the closest vowel that stands between two consonants from the right side of the word.\n    Vowels at the beginning or ending don't count.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(word) < 3:\n        return \"\"\n\n    for i in range(len(word) - 2, 0, -1):\n        current_char = word[i]\n        \n        if _is_vowel(current_char):\n            left_char = word[i-1]\n            right_char = word[i+1]\n            \n            if _is_consonant(left_char) and _is_consonant(right_char):\n                return current_char\n\n    return \"\""}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/121", "solution": "```python\ndef solution(lst):\n    \"\"\"Given a non-empty list of integers, return the sum of all of the odd elements that are in even positions.\n    Positions are 0-based, so even positions are at indices 0, 2, 4, etc.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for index, element in enumerate(lst):\n        # Sums the element if its index is even and the element itself is odd.\n        if index % 2 == 0 and element % 2 != 0:\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/120", "solution": "import heapq\n\ndef maximum(arr, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a sorted list of length k with the maximum k numbers in arr.\n    Assumes 0 <= k <= len(arr) and arr contains only integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if k == 0:\n        return []\n    # Use heapq.nlargest to efficiently find the k largest elements.\n    # The result is then sorted ascending as required.\n    top_k = heapq.nlargest(k, arr)\n    return sorted(top_k)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/119", "solution": "```python\n# src/paren_matcher/__init__.py\nfrom .api import match_parens\n\n# Re-export top-level API function\n__all__ = [\"match_parens\"]\n\n# src/paren_matcher/api.py\nfrom typing import Sequence\nfrom .utils.validator import Validator\nfrom .services.matcher import MatcherService\nfrom .repos.cache import CacheRepository\nfrom .config import AppConfig, DEFAULT_CONFIG\nfrom .models.result import ResultDTO\n\n# Global instances for API level to ensure consistent cache and config\n_validator = Validator(config=DEFAULT_CONFIG)\n_cache_repo = CacheRepository()\n_matcher_service = MatcherService(cache_repo=_cache_repo, config=DEFAULT_CONFIG)\n\ndef match_parens(inputs: Sequence[str]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Programmatic API to check if two parenthesis-only strings can be concatenated\n    in some order to form a balanced parentheses string.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validator.validate_inputs(inputs)\n    result_dto = _matcher_service.check(inputs)\n    return result_dto.as_str()\n\n# src/paren_matcher/cli.py\nimport sys\nimport argparse\nfrom typing import Sequence, Optional\n\nfrom ..exceptions import ParenMatcherException\nfrom ..services.matcher import MatcherService\nfrom ..repos.cache import CacheRepository\nfrom ..config import AppConfig, DEFAULT_CONFIG\nfrom ..utils.validator import Validator\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"CLI controller for the paren-match command.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: MatcherService, validator: Validator) -> None:\n        self._service = service\n        self._validator = validator\n\n    def run(self, argv: Sequence[str]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Executes the CLI command logic.\n        Parses arguments, calls the service, and prints the result or error.\n        \"\"\"\n        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n            description=\"Check if two parenthesis strings can form a balanced string.\",\n            prog=\"paren-match\"\n        )\n        parser.add_argument('string1', help='First parenthesis string.')\n        parser.add_argument('string2', help='Second parenthesis string.')\n\n        try:\n            args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n            \n            inputs_for_service = [args.string1, args.string2]\n            self._validator.validate_inputs(inputs_for_service)\n            \n            result_dto = self._service.check(inputs_for_service)\n            print(result_dto.as_str())\n            return 0\n        except ParenMatcherException as e:\n            print(f\"Error: {e.message}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[Sequence[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Main entry point for the CLI script.\n    Uses module-level singletons for consistency and caching.\n    \"\"\"\n    if argv is None:\n        argv = sys.argv[1:]\n\n    _cache_repo_cli = CacheRepository()\n    _matcher_service_cli = MatcherService(cache_repo=_cache_repo_cli, config=DEFAULT_CONFIG)\n    _validator_cli = Validator(config=DEFAULT_CONFIG)\n\n    controller = CLIController(service=_matcher_service_cli, validator=_validator_cli)\n    return controller.run(argv)\n\n# src/paren_matcher/config.py\nfrom typing import Tuple\n\nclass AppConfig:\n    \"\"\"Centralized configuration values for the application.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.min_python_version: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 9)\n        self.allowed_chars: str = '()'\n\nDEFAULT_CONFIG = AppConfig()\n\n# src/paren_matcher/exceptions.py\nclass ParenMatcherException(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for ParenMatcher errors.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str) -> None:\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n\n# src/paren_matcher/models/result.py\nclass ResultDTO:\n    \"\"\"Data Transfer Object for results.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, result: str) -> None:\n        self.result = result\n\n    def as_str(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Returns the result as a string.\"\"\"\n        return self.result\n\n# src/paren_matcher/repos/cache.py\nfrom typing import Dict, Tuple, Optional\n\nclass CacheRepository:\n    \"\"\"Simple in-memory cache for results.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._store: Dict[Tuple[str, str], str] = {}\n\n    def get(self, key: Tuple[str, str]) -> Optional[str]:\n        \"\"\"Retrieves a value from the cache by key.\"\"\"\n        return self._store.get(key)\n\n    def set(self, key: Tuple[str, str], value: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Stores a value in the cache with the given key.\"\"\"\n        self._store[key] = value\n\n    def clear(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Clears all entries from the cache.\"\"\"\n        self._store.clear()\n\n# src/paren_matcher/services/matcher.py\nfrom typing import Sequence, Tuple, Optional\nfrom ..repos.cache import CacheRepository\nfrom ..models.result import ResultDTO\nfrom ..config import AppConfig, DEFAULT_CONFIG\n\nclass MatcherService:\n    \"\"\"Core logic for checking balanced parentheses concatenation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, cache_repo: Optional[CacheRepository] = None, config: Optional[AppConfig] = None) -> None:\n        self._cache = cache_repo if cache_repo is not None else CacheRepository()\n        self._config = config if config is not None else DEFAULT_CONFIG\n\n    def check(self, inputs: Sequence[str]) -> ResultDTO:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if two strings can be concatenated in some order to form a balanced parentheses string.\n        Assumes inputs are already validated.\n        \"\"\"\n        s1, s2 = inputs[0], inputs[1]\n\n        # Check cache for both permutations\n        cached_result = self._cache.get((s1, s2))\n        if cached_result is None:\n            cached_result = self._cache.get((s2, s1))\n        \n        if cached_result is not None:\n            return ResultDTO(cached_result)\n\n        # Compute properties for both strings\n        bal1, min_pref1 = self._string_props(s1)\n        bal2, min_pref2 = self._string_props(s2)\n\n        # Check both concatenation orders\n        can_concat_s1_s2 = self._can_concat(bal1, min_pref1, bal2, min_pref2)\n        can_concat_s2_s1 = self._can_concat(bal2, min_pref2, bal1, min_pref1)\n\n        result_str = 'Yes' if can_concat_s1_s2 or can_concat_s2_s1 else 'No'\n\n        # Store result in cache for both permutations as per design\n        self._cache.set((s1, s2), result_str)\n        if s1 != s2: # Avoid redundant storage if strings are identical\n            self._cache.set((s2, s1), result_str)\n        \n        return ResultDTO(result_str)\n\n    def _string_props(self, s: str) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Scans a string to compute its final balance and minimum prefix sum.\n        Balance is (open_count - close_count).\n        \"\"\"\n        balance = 0\n        min_prefix = 0\n        for char in s:\n            if char == '(':\n                balance += 1\n            else:\n                balance -= 1\n            min_prefix = min(min_prefix, balance)\n        return balance, min_prefix\n\n    def _can_concat(self, bal_a: int, min_pref_a: int, bal_b: int, min_pref_b: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if a string with properties (bal_a, min_pref_a) followed by\n        another (bal_b, min_pref_b) results in a balanced string.\n        \"\"\"\n        return (min_pref_a >= 0) and (min_pref_b + bal_a >= 0) and (bal_a + bal_b == 0)\n\n# src/paren_matcher/utils/validator.py\nfrom typing import Sequence\nfrom ..exceptions import ParenMatcherException\nfrom ..config import AppConfig, DEFAULT_CONFIG\n\nclass Validator:\n    \"\"\"Validates inputs according to business rules.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, config: AppConfig = DEFAULT_CONFIG) -> None:\n        self._config = config\n\n    def validate_inputs(self, inputs: Sequence[str]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates the input sequence of strings.\n        Raises ParenMatcherException for invalid inputs.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(inputs, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ParenMatcherException(\"Input must be a list or tuple.\")\n\n        if len(inputs) != 2:\n            raise ParenMatcherException(\"Input must contain exactly two strings.\")\n\n        for i, s in enumerate(inputs):\n            if not isinstance(s, str):\n                raise ParenMatcherException(f\"Input element at index {i} must be a string.\")\n            for char in s:\n                if char not in self._config.allowed_chars:\n                    raise ParenMatcherException(\n                        f\"String '{s}' contains invalid character '{char}'. Only '(' and ')' are allowed.\"\n                    )\n\n```\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"paren-matcher\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A Python library to check if parenthesis strings can form a balanced string.\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\nlicense = { file = \"LICENSE\" }\nauthors = [\n  { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\nclassifiers = [\n    \"Programming Language :: Python :: 3\",\n    \"License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\",\n    \"Operating System :: OS Independent\",\n]\n\n[project.urls]\nHomepage = \"https://github.com/yourusername/paren-matcher\" # Placeholder\nRepository = \"https://github.com/yourusername/paren-matcher\" # Placeholder\n\n[project.scripts]\nparen-match = \"paren_matcher.cli:main\"\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\n    \"pytest>=7.0\",\n    \"flake8>=6.0\",\n    \"black>=23.0\",\n]\n```\n```markdown\n# README.md\n# paren-matcher\n\nA Python library and command-line tool to determine if two parenthesis-only strings can be concatenated in some order to form a balanced parentheses string.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\npip install paren-matcher # (Once published to PyPI)\n# Or, for local development:\n# git clone https://github.com/yourusername/paren-matcher.git\n# cd paren-matcher\n# pip install -e .\n# pip install -e \".[dev]\" # For development dependencies like pytest\n```\n\n## Usage\n\n### Programmatic API\n\nYou can use the `match_parens` function in your Python code:\n\n```python\nfrom paren_matcher import match_parens\n\n# Example 1: Can form a balanced string\nresult = match_parens(['()(', ')'])\nprint(f\"Result for ['()(', ')']: {result}\") # Expected: Yes\n\n# Example 2: Cannot form a balanced string\nresult = match_parens(['))', '('])\nprint(f\"Result for ['))', '(']: {result}\") # Expected: No\n\n# Example 3: Edge case with empty strings (balanced, so 'Yes')\nresult = match_parens(['', '()'])\nprint(f\"Result for ['', '()']: {result}\") # Expected: Yes\n\n# Example 4: Invalid input (raises ParenMatcherException)\ntry:\n    match_parens(['()']) # Needs exactly two strings\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n### Command-Line Interface (CLI)\n\nThe library provides a `paren-match` command-line tool:\n\n```bash\nparen-match '()(' ')'\n# Output: Yes\n\nparen-match '))' '('\n# Output: No\n\nparen-match '' '()'\n# Output: Yes\n\n# Invalid input handling\nparen-match '(()'\n# Output: Error: Input must contain exactly two strings.\n\nparen-match '(()' 'a)'\n# Output: Error: String 'a)' contains invalid character 'a'. Only '(' and ')' are allowed.\n```\n\n## Development\n\nTo run tests and contribute:\n\n```bash\npip install -e \".[dev]\"\npytest\n```\n\n## License\n\nThis project is licensed under the MIT License - see the [LICENSE](LICENSE) file for details.\n```\n```text\n# LICENSE\nMIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2023 Your Name\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/120", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef maximum(arr, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the k largest numbers from arr, sorted in non-decreasing order.\n    Assumes valid integer inputs as per problem constraints.\n    \"\"\"\n    if k == 0:\n        return []\n    \n    # Efficiently finds the k largest elements from the array.\n    top_k_elements = heapq.nlargest(k, arr)\n    \n    # Sorts the found elements in non-decreasing order as required.\n    return sorted(top_k_elements)\n```"}
